WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:02.949
Film Courage: In under 5 minutes can you give
us the best screenwriting advice?

00:00:02.949 --> 00:00:06.109
Gavin Michael Booth, Writer/Director/Cinematographer:
Be different.

00:00:06.109 --> 00:00:11.600
Just because you read a book on screenwriting,
don’t follow that exact format.

00:00:11.600 --> 00:00:13.640
Read every screenplays.

00:00:13.640 --> 00:00:19.810
Read every screenplay you can from amateur
to professional.

00:00:19.810 --> 00:00:25.019
Movies you’ve watched, get those screenplays,
especially from sites like (I don’t even

00:00:25.019 --> 00:00:29.290
know who still exists) but Drew’s Script-o-rama
or anywhere that houses screenplays but especially

00:00:29.290 --> 00:00:32.560
if you live in LA there’s stores that sell
them.

00:00:32.560 --> 00:00:37.400
I try to get early drafts to scripts and understand
the changes that they’ve made to production.

00:00:37.400 --> 00:00:44.751
I think the more you see the evolution of
how writing is changing over the years, how

00:00:44.751 --> 00:00:51.930
character development and dialogue and looking
at all of the different…because there isn’t

00:00:51.930 --> 00:00:54.400
one set format for writing a screenplay.

00:00:54.400 --> 00:00:56.530
There used to be but not so much anymore.

00:00:56.530 --> 00:01:01.190
I just feel like the more you read, you just
filter through this works for what I’m doing,

00:01:01.190 --> 00:01:06.890
this doesn’t work, when you just see the
way that people use description, it’s just

00:01:06.890 --> 00:01:07.890
practice.

00:01:07.890 --> 00:01:11.160
For indie filmmakers, don’t shoot your first
draft.

00:01:11.160 --> 00:01:18.460
Your first draft is not perfect unless you
are the next Robert Town or just somebody

00:01:18.460 --> 00:01:22.990
who’s got this amazing ability to…Kravin
Smythe of CLARKS (but I bet that wasn’t

00:01:22.990 --> 00:01:24.830
even his first draft).

00:01:24.830 --> 00:01:29.740
Just because you finish it and doesn’t mean
it’s perfect, so get feedback.

00:01:29.740 --> 00:01:34.550
Have table reads, listen to the advice people
give you even if you don’t like it.

00:01:34.550 --> 00:01:37.380
You are always going to have to fight for
some things and you don’t have to take everyone’s

00:01:37.380 --> 00:01:38.380
advice.

00:01:38.380 --> 00:01:42.130
You will never not learn something that you
didn’t think of or didn’t see as being

00:01:42.130 --> 00:01:47.229
a problem in your script the more people you
ask to give you feedback.

00:01:47.229 --> 00:01:50.610
Of course you will want to do something weird
and wild that people won’t understand and

00:01:50.610 --> 00:01:56.830
that’s okay you don’t have to change but
you have to rewrite and rewrite and rewrite

00:01:56.830 --> 00:02:01.440
to get that feedback because it’s so important
to shape…there is no way that any one writer

00:02:01.440 --> 00:02:07.549
can have all the answers and bang out a perfect
screenplay without that guidance and help.

00:02:07.549 --> 00:02:10.369
Film Courage: Did you ever think about what
your life would be like if you hadn’t have

00:02:10.369 --> 00:02:12.069
gone to that Third Eye Blind concert?

00:02:12.069 --> 00:02:16.750
Gavin: I’d probably be shooting wedding
videos in Windsor, Ontario [Canada].

00:02:16.750 --> 00:02:22.730
Yes, I’m not sure…I think I’ve got the
fight in me and I’ve wanted to make movies

00:02:22.730 --> 00:02:26.030
for a long time and I’ve always figured
out a way.

00:02:26.030 --> 00:02:29.450
It could be that it’s nothing you can’t
think about right?

00:02:29.450 --> 00:02:33.980
Because it’s that sliding doors effect of
the Gwines Paltros movie of the BUTTERFLY

00:02:33.980 --> 00:02:34.980
RIPPLES.

00:02:34.980 --> 00:02:40.209
If I’d gone a more traditional way would
I be making more experimental movies I’m

00:02:40.209 --> 00:02:41.209
making now?

00:02:41.209 --> 00:02:42.209
Probably not.

00:02:42.209 --> 00:02:46.690
Maybe I’d have more success working in the
studio system or for television networks but

00:02:46.690 --> 00:02:48.900
it would be wildly different.

00:02:48.900 --> 00:02:53.852
And that just taught me to start sneaking
into everything as long as I wasn’t breaking

00:02:53.852 --> 00:02:54.852
the law.

00:02:54.852 --> 00:02:57.280
I met my wife sneaking into a party at the
Toronto Film Festival.

00:02:57.280 --> 00:03:01.650
I definitely would not have done that if it
had not worked previously so successful with

00:03:01.650 --> 00:03:07.410
bands and I used to sneak into events where
I knew that I could meet people that couldn’t

00:03:07.410 --> 00:03:10.340
potentially read scripts or take a meeting
with.

00:03:10.340 --> 00:03:11.920
So yes…it would be wildly different.

